# WithRunContext quick fix can emit a call with no matching overload when userdata is a bool variable

## Steps to Reproduce

1. TODO: Add steps

## Expected Behavior

TODO: Describe expected behavior

## Actual Behavior

TODO: Describe actual behavior

Found and documented by the B047/B048 lane, 2026-08-30, which withheld the
fix for the syntactically-detectable cases and flagged the remainder rather
than shipping it silently. Verified independently by the coordinator against
the shipped type DB.

## Defect
`startnew` and `Meta::StartWithRunContext` do NOT have matching overload
sets. From `tests/fixtures/typedb/OpenplanetCore.json`:

- `startnew`: **9** overloads, including
  `awaitable@ startnew(CoroutineFuncUserdataBool@ func, const bool userdata)`
- `StartWithRunContext`: **8** overloads, with **no bool counterpart**
  (ref, double, int, int64, string, uint, uint64 — no bool)

So rewriting `startnew(f, <bool>).WithRunContext(ctx)` into
`Meta::StartWithRunContext(ctx, f, <bool>)` produces a call that does not
exist.

B048 withholds the fix when the userdata is **syntactically** boolean — a
`true`/`false` literal, `!x`, or a comparison/logical operator. A bool
**variable** (`bool flag; startnew(f, flag).WithRunContext(ctx)`) is not
detectable that way and still gets the fix.

## Why it matters
The repo's own rule for this family is 'never offer a fix you cannot make'.
This is the one case that breaks it: the user applies a quick fix and the
result does not compile. It is a LOUD failure — a compile error, not silent
data loss — which is why B048 shipped rather than blocking, and that call was
right. But a quick fix emitting non-compiling code is still a defect.

## Work
- Closing this properly needs **overload resolution inside a code-action
  producer**, which is why B048 did not attempt it: the producer would have to
  know the static type of the userdata expression, not just its syntax.
- Check whether the type checker's existing call-site machinery
  (`src/typecheck/call_site.rs`, the B010/B023 work) can be reached from a
  code-action producer, or whether the diagnostic should carry the resolved
  argument type forward so the producer does not re-derive it. The second is
  likely cheaper and matches how B036/B037 pass `replacement` through.
- Alternative if that is too invasive: withhold the fix whenever the userdata
  expression is anything other than a literal of a known-good type. That
  trades false-withholding for correctness, which is the right direction for
  an auto-applicable fix, but it makes the fix much rarer — decide
  deliberately rather than by default.
- Confirm against the live game that a bool userdata really is rejected,
  rather than being silently coerced. Probe before implementing; this repo has
  twice had a data-derived prediction turn out wrong.

## Test plan (TDD, red first)
Red: `bool flag; startnew(F, flag).WithRunContext(ctx);` currently offers a
fix whose result has no matching overload.

Variants (per CLAUDE.md, generalize):
- bool literal, `!x`, comparison, logical operator -> already withheld, must
  stay withheld
- bool variable, bool function return, bool field, bool array element
- a `ref` userdata, which DOES have a counterpart -> must still be offered
- each of the 7 types that do have counterparts -> still offered
- a userdata whose type cannot be resolved at all -> withheld

## Gate
Code actions add no diagnostics, so FP fixtures must be byte-identical.
Measure two binaries on the same machine at the same time.

## Related
B048 (shipped the fix and documented this gap at the site), B047 (the seam),
B036 (the RemovedApiUsage family that had no live fix until B048), B023, B010.

## Closed by B048, 2026-08-30 — verified, not assumed

Fixed in the same lane, in commit `5d3ad34` ("withhold unsafe run-context
rewrites"), landed via the B047/B048 merge `281f859`.

The first B048 implementation tested the userdata SYNTACTICALLY (literal,
`!x`, comparison, logical operator), which is what left a bool VARIABLE
slipping through — the gap this ticket recorded. The final guard inverts the
question: `userdata_has_successor_type()` asks the CHECKER to prove the
expression's recorded type is one of the seven `StartWithRunContext` actually
accepts, rather than trying to enumerate boolean syntax.

That is STRICTER than this ticket asked for. It withholds:
- a bool variable (`flag`) — the exact case filed here
- a bool-returning call (`IsReady()`)
- a constructor-style cast (`bool(1)`) and `cast<bool>(flag)`
- a ternary (`flag ? true : false`)
- **any expression whose type cannot be resolved at all** — it has not proved
  that any successor overload accepts it, so the safe answer is no edit

Verified in the merged tree rather than taken on report:
`every_semantically_boolean_userdata_shape_offers_nothing` iterates all five
shapes above, and `an_unproved_userdata_type_offers_nothing` covers the
unresolved case. Both assert the DIAGNOSTIC still fires while no fix is
offered — withholding the fix never hides the finding.

The route this ticket predicted would be cheaper (carry the resolved argument
type forward on the diagnostic) was not needed; the family reaches the checker
through `FixContext` directly.

No probe was required after all: the overload asymmetry is readable from the
shipped type DB (`OpenplanetCore.json`, ns `Meta`) — `startnew` has 9
overloads including `CoroutineFuncUserdataBool@ func, const bool userdata`
and `StartWithRunContext` has 8 with no bool counterpart — and the fix is to
withhold rather than to emit, so no claim about game acceptance is being
made.